---
// „How Matrix was built“: wie Matrix mit Claude Code als Orchestrierung vieler Agenten entstand, mit Regeln, Episoden aus dem
// Git-Log und Messungen. Jede Zahl ist belegt; die Fundstellen stehen in den Kommentaren je Abschnitt (Stand 2026-11-25).
// Umfangszahlen per Zählskript aus den getrackten Dateien von ~/matrix, ~/matrix/app, ~/matrix/core (ohne vendorten Code).
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import MatrixArchitecture from '../../../components/diagrams/MatrixArchitecture.astro';
import '../../../styles/story.css';
import { SITE } from '../../../site';
const NPM = 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/@matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp';
const REPO = 'https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp';
// Video-Plätze: nur in `astro dev` sichtbar, bis die Videos gebaut sind (Skill `videos`). Danach durch <Video> ersetzen.
const SHOW_VIDEO_SLOTS = import.meta.env.DEV;
---
<Base wide title="How Matrix was built" description="How I built Matrix, a local document app, with Claude Code agents: the rules, where they came from, the checks before anything reaches me, and what I measured.">
<div class="story">

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in read">
      <p class="kicker">Matrix LLM · how it was built</p>
      <h1>How I built Matrix with Claude Code.</h1>
      <p class="big">Almost all of Matrix was written by Claude Code, in many sessions and agents working in parallel. My part was the
      rules they work under, the checks before anything reaches me, and the decisions. This page shows those rules, where they
      came from, and what I measured along the way.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">The app</p>
        <h2>What Matrix is.</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="big">Matrix answers questions about your own documents, recordings and videos, and every answer cites the
        exact passage. Plain code checks each citation against the original. The models run on your devices, several of your
        devices can share them, and Claude can use Matrix as a tool through MCP.</p>
        <div class="get">
          <a class="badge" href={SITE.matrixApp}><img src="/badges/download-on-the-app-store.svg" width="120" height="40" alt="Download on the App Store" /></a>
          <a class="badge" href={SITE.matrixWin}><img src="/badges/get-it-from-microsoft.svg" width="161" height="44" alt="Get it from Microsoft" /></a>
        </div>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1rem">Claude Code plugin: <a href={REPO}>matrix-mcp on GitHub</a> ·
        <a href={NPM}>@matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp on npm</a> · <a href="/projects/matrix/">the app</a> ·
        <a href="/projects/matrix-claude-code/">the plugin</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Umfang: Zählskript 2026-11-25. Commits: git rev-list --count HEAD (Doku 978, app 823, core 243); Claude-Trailer:
       app 794, core 224. Rust: core 44.035 + app 191.711 Zeilen; TS/TSX 61.914; Tests: #[test]/#[tokio::test] core 791 +
       app 2.916, Vitest-Fälle 1.541; Handbuch 8.161 Zeilen; Oberflächensprachen 22 (23 Kataloge minus Pseudo-Sprache en-XA). -->
  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Size</p>
      <h2>Five and a half months, counted.</h2>
      <div class="numbers">
        <div><strong>2,044</strong><span>commits in three repositories since 6 June 2026; 1,018 of the 1,066 code commits are co-authored by Claude</span></div>
        <div><strong>236,000</strong><span>lines of Rust, plus 62,000 lines of TypeScript for the interface</span></div>
        <div><strong>5,248</strong><span>automated tests: 3,707 in Rust, 1,541 for the interface</span></div>
        <div><strong>8,161</strong><span>lines of handbook that every session reads from</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:2rem">Counted on 25 November 2026 from the files under version control, without vendored code.
      Lines include comments and blank lines. A test is one test function or case, so the number says nothing about coverage.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quelle: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md „Mach X heißt: orchestriere X“, „Fixes bündeln“; ~/matrix/CLAUDE.md „Tests“, „Verifikation“. -->
  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The setup</p>
      <h2>One session talks to me. The others do the work.</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><div><h3>The main session coordinates</h3><p>It talks with me and stays free for that. It does small things itself, under
        about a minute. Everything bigger goes to a worker: a separate session if I want to steer it, a background agent otherwise.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>One package, one worker</h3><p>Design, build and fixes of a package stay with the same agent, because every hand-over
        loses knowledge. A worker splits off only what is independent, produces a lot of output (builds, logs, wide searches) or needs
        fresh eyes (acceptance, review).</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>The model is chosen, not inherited</h3><p>Sonnet for mechanical work such as running builds and collecting material,
        Opus for building and judging. When a machine checks the result (an exit code, a count), the agent runs with low effort and only
        reports red. It does not go looking for the cause; an Opus agent does the diagnosis.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>"Green" in a report is not enough</h3><p>The main session runs the builds and tests of an agent again itself. A commit
        happens only after the test's own exit code is green, never after something like <code>cargo test | grep</code>.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>An acceptance agent before I see it</h3><p>Before anything goes to me for testing, a separate agent uses every changed
        function the way I would: the real interface in the iOS Simulator or the web version, two instances when devices talk to each other.
        Only when that is green is the build installed on my devices.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Fixes are bundled</h3><p>While known bugs are open, each fix runs only its own tests and is committed locally. The full
        checks, one push (one CI run) and the installation happen once at the end of the round. A red main branch on GitHub is fixed at once.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quelle: ~/matrix/CLAUDE.md „Handbuch pflegen“, „Tests“, „Verifikation“; Hooks ~/matrix/.claude/hooks/ (rust-check, gates-check,
       ci-check); scripts/check_file_size.py (600), check_i18n.py; gen_file_briefs.py. -->
  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Memory</p>
      <h2>The rules live in the repository, not in my head.</h2>
      <p class="big">An agent starts with no memory of yesterday. So everything it needs is written down where it will read it, and what
      can be enforced is enforced by a script.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>A short rule file</h3><p>Every session loads a CLAUDE.md of under 200 lines and the handbook index, then exactly one area
        file of the handbook, never all of them.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Wrong facts are fixed at once</h3><p>If an agent finds the handbook contradicting the code, it fixes the handbook in the
        same step. A wrong entry would mislead every later session.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Find before you build</h3><p>Every source file starts with a comment on what it does. A generated index of these lets an
        agent find existing code before it writes a second copy.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Tests per user function</h3><p>Including whole flows across devices: device A offers, B pairs, B picks, the question runs
        on A. A bug I find gets a test that reproduces it first.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Hooks that block</h3><p>A session cannot end while the Rust checks, the project gates or the CI run on the main branch are
        red. Gates also catch source files over 600 lines (except a short list, each with its own cap) and interface text missing in
        any of the 22 languages.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The same in every session</h3><p>When I give a rule that applies to the whole project, it goes into that file right away,
        not into a single conversation.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quellen: app da38745d (2026-11-09), 6e17a225 (11-22), abea1a8e (11-21); Doku 258d784 (11-24); Doku 6b8e56c (11-21);
       app adeb077d, 82439df6 (11-22, Abnahme); app 3a766a6c + core 59ee0fd (Windows-Absturz); app 5b2f28cc (Nutzerbefund 11-24). -->
  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Where the rules came from</p>
      <h2>Most rules are the answer to something that went wrong.</h2>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>A commit took another session's work</h3><p>On 9 November an agent committed unfinished changes of a parallel session
        along with its own; the next commit put them back. Similar slips happened twice more in the next two weeks. Rule: never <code>git add -A</code>
        when there are changes you did not make.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Checks that said green on an empty page</h3><p>The interface checks passed although the web version delivered no app at
        all. Now a check is red unless every view was really measured, and an empty page counts as a finding.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Only I saw the red CI</h3><p>A failed GitHub run used to reach me by e-mail and nobody else. Since 21 November a hook
        reports it to the session, which has to fix it before it can stop.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The acceptance agent found what tests did not</h3><p>Unpairing on the Mac never reached the iPhone; after the fix it
        arrives in 0.8 seconds. "Who was Goethe? Answer in two sentences." got the app's help pages and a refusal, because everyday words
        counted as questions about the app.</p></div>
        <div><h3>A crash, first reproduced</h3><p>From its second start on, the Windows app crashed after 5 to 15 seconds: three threads set
        up the graphics card at the same time. The test reproduces it. On the PC, 8 of 10 runs crashed before the fix, 15 of 15 passed after.</p></div>
        <div><h3>My own test, then a test</h3><p>A question from my iPhone to the Mac's 35B model came back as "this device is busy",
        although the Mac had nothing loaded. The fix came with tests for exactly that case and for the error message.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:2rem">Not every rule has a recorded incident. Most rule commits hold only the new text, so
      this list has only cases where a commit names the cause.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quellen: ~/matrix/handbuch/medien-messungen.md (Alltagston, Folientitel, 2026-11-24); mcp-messungen.md (Reranker-Kandidaten
       2026-11-04, Lizenz 2026-10-23); app 96dcab42 (2026-11-21); Doku d8e842c (2026-11-20); app 6d481e65 (2026-07-12);
       peer-messungen.md (2026-11-21); core.md „Plätze“ (1,20× unter 1,3×). -->
  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Measure, don't guess</p>
      <h2>A measurement decides, and often against my first idea.</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="demo">
          <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Measured</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Is Apple's built-in speech recognition good enough, or does the iPhone need Whisper?</td><td>19.6 % word errors against 18.8 % for Whisper turbo, on 95 recordings (20.6 minutes) of accents, Swiss German and street noise</td><td>Apple's text is enough on the iPhone; Whisper turbo stays the default elsewhere</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Does skipping the silence detection help Whisper?</td><td>Worse: 220 instead of 187 wrong words, and more invented words in noisy files</td><td>Keep it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Should the re-ranker read the whole passage instead of the first 512 characters?</td><td>27 more hits, none fewer, across four test tables (McNemar p &lt; 0.001); 1.05 instead of 0.64 seconds per question</td><td>Whole passage</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Do more candidates for the re-ranker find more?</td><td>120 candidates: MRR 0.645 in 6.6 s; 20 candidates: 0.680 in 1.3 s</td><td>20</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Which re-ranker for the public plugin?</td><td>jina scored best (MRR 0.69), but its licence is non-commercial; bge int8 0.63, same as its full-size version at a quarter of the size</td><td>bge, slower but free to use</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Is Apple's document recognition worth adding for scans?</td><td>6 questions better, 5 worse, for about 1,100 lines and a Swift bridge</td><td>Removed again</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Do slide titles in a talk's transcript help search?</td><td>One question won, one lost. The rule written down before the run: only with at least 4 more</td><td>Without</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Does a second device speed up tables?</td><td>Mac and PC together were only 6 % faster than the PC alone</td><td>Rebuilt the split: 16 % now; embedding on both is 41 % faster than the best single device</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Do parallel table rows on one model help?</td><td>1.20× in the self-test, under the 1.3× threshold</td><td>One row at a time, on every device today</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Does a fourth tool help the local model?</td><td>Its mere presence in the prompt broke a hard check; the model never called it in 9 runs</td><td>Removed</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">MRR (mean reciprocal rank) is 1 if the right passage is always the first hit, 0.5 if it is
      always second. All runs on a Mac M4 Pro or one PC (Ryzen 7 7800X3D, Radeon RX 7900 XTX), July to November 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quelle: ~/matrix/handbuch/messen.md „Testbestände“, „Methodenprüfung vor langen Läufen“, „Vergleichen“;
       ~/matrix/CLAUDE.md „Messläufe“. -->
  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">How the measuring works</p>
      <h2>The agents measure. The method is checked first.</h2>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Answers fixed in advance</h3><p>For every test question, the passage it should find is chosen from the raw text before the
        app has seen the question.</p></div>
        <div><h3>A second agent checks the method</h3><p>Before any run over about 30 minutes, an agent that did not pick the candidates
        checks a list: same model family, same settings, hidden caps in the code, a seed that really changes between repetitions.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Why that list exists</h3><p>A hidden cap limits a result without anyone noticing. A fixed seed turns five repetitions
        into five copies of the same run.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Never in parallel when timing</h3><p>On Apple chips two model runs share the memory bandwidth and the times roughly double.
        Ranks and quality stay the same, so only timed runs have to wait.</p></div>
        <div><h3>One change per comparison</h3><p>And the noise is named: with 30 questions, one or two ranks are chance.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The real app as a sample</h3><p>Every series of headless model runs also gets 3 to 5 of its questions through the built app,
        because the interface shares memory and graphics card with the model.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quelle: ~/matrix/app/claude-plugin/DEMO.md (Plugin 0.2.9, 2026-11-19, 4 Fragen × 2 Läufe, 442 Seiten). -->
  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">One result</p>
      <h2>Claude Code with and without Matrix.</h2>
      <p class="big">Four questions on 442 pages of EU regulations and an ENISA report, each asked twice, with Claude Code alone and with
      the Matrix plugin (Sonnet, 19 November 2026).</p>
      <div class="numbers">
        <div><strong>20 s</strong><span>per question with Matrix, 48 s without</span></div>
        <div><strong>182k</strong><span>input tokens per question with Matrix, 1,025k without (both including cache)</span></div>
        <div><strong>8 of 8</strong><span>answers right with Matrix, 7 of 8 without</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:2rem">Eight runs are no proof of better answers; the gain is in time and tokens. The set and the
      script are public: <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/benchmark`}>benchmark on GitHub</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quelle: ~/matrix/HANDBUCH.md §1.0, §2; handbuch/peer.md „Schichten“. -->
  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Architecture</p>
      <h2>One Rust core, three ways in.</h2>
      <div class="card"><MatrixArchitecture /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {SHOW_VIDEO_SLOTS && (
    <section class="beat">
      <div class="in">
        <p class="kicker">Videos (draft slots, visible only in dev)</p>
        <h2>Three short videos.</h2>
        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="slot"><h3>1 · A round of work</h3><p>Proposal: I ask the main session for a feature; it starts one worker, the
          progress bar fills, the acceptance agent runs the iOS Simulator, the result arrives. Real screen recording, sped up, about 60 s.</p></div>
          <div class="slot"><h3>2 · A check that fails</h3><p>Proposal: the stop hook blocks a session because the CI is red; the agent
          fixes it and only then ends. About 30 s.</p></div>
          <div class="slot"><h3>3 · A measurement that changed a decision</h3><p>Proposal: the re-ranker comparison (512 characters vs.
          whole passage), from the run to the commit with the numbers. About 45 s.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in read">
      <p class="kicker">Limits</p>
      <h2>What this page does not show.</h2>
      <p class="big">The numbers come from my own devices and test sets of 30 questions or a few dozen recordings, not from many users.
      The rules grew one problem at a time, so some of them came after the damage. I don't read the code line by line any more; I read the tests, the measurements and
      the app in my hands. And a test count says how much is checked, not how well.</p>
      <a class="go ghost" href="/projects/matrix/">Back to Matrix</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</Base>

